{"iter": 0, "slug": "baseline", "ts": "2026-05-18T06:12:51.502804Z", "status": "kept", "family": "baseline", "composite": 0.85, "composite_delta": 0.0, "sub_scores": {"strict_format": 1.0, "applies_cleanly": 0.8667, "target_intent_captured": 0.7667, "minimal_changes": 0.8667}, "training": {}, "regressions": [], "headroom_used": 0.0, "headroom_remaining": 0.15, "notes": "Baseline 0.1883. Target = applies_cleanly (0.40 weight \u00d7 0.800 = 0.320 headroom, 65% of total movable 0.812). 4B has the capability in seed form (23% strict_format) but fails often. With the strict-cascade rubric from cap #4's lesson and 4 anti-shortcut sub-scores, this is well-positioned for OPD. RE-SCORED with corrected rubric that accepts ```diff fenced wrapper. The 4B IS good at diffs in its natural fenced format; the original strict-only-bare-diff rubric mis-measured baseline by -66pp."}
{"iter": 1, "slug": "h1-r16-2ep", "ts": "2026-05-18T06:19:59.650700Z", "status": "discarded", "family": "H1", "target_sub_score": "target_intent_captured", "hypothesis": "Proven recipe lifts applies_cleanly \u226510pp and composite \u22654pp.", "verdict": "\u2717 CATASTROPHIC \u2014 composite collapsed from baseline 0.8500 to 0.0983 (-75.17pp). Only 8 effective steps but each gradient was huge (loss range 5-28) due to low student-teacher rollout overlap. The 8 noisy updates destroyed the LoRA. Note: original strict-format-only rubric had me thinking baseline was 0.19 and iter 1 was 0.085 \u2014 misdiagnosis; correcting the rubric showed iter 1 actually wrecked a 0.85 model.", "composite": 0.0983, "composite_delta": -0.7517, "headroom_used": -5.011, "sub_scores": {"strict_format": 0.2333, "applies_cleanly": 0.1, "target_intent_captured": 0.05, "minimal_changes": 0.1}, "regressions": [{"sub_score": "strict_format", "delta": -0.7667, "severity": "catastrophic"}, {"sub_score": "applies_cleanly", "delta": -0.7667, "severity": "catastrophic"}, {"sub_score": "target_intent_captured", "delta": -0.7167, "severity": "catastrophic"}, {"sub_score": "minimal_changes", "delta": -0.7667, "severity": "catastrophic"}], "training": {"rank": 16, "alpha": 32, "lr": 0.0001, "epochs": 2, "samples_per_prompt": 1, "max_tokens": 512, "top_k": 8, "effective_steps": 8, "nominal_steps": 48, "skip_rate": 0.833, "elapsed_s": 71}, "asi": {"what_worked": "The kiln checkpoint+adapter pipeline ran cleanly end-to-end. Training finished in 71s (fast). The rubric_sanity calibration gate that caught my initial Goodhart hole worked. The all-zeros gate per skill \u00a710 should have fired (0.0983 < 0.5\u00d70.8500 = 0.425) \u2014 and indeed it would have, with the corrected rubric.", "what_failed": "TWO failures interacted: (1) Eval-design failure UNDER-estimated baseline (0.19 vs actual 0.85) by rejecting fenced diffs. The 4B's natural output convention is ```diff\\n<diff>\\n```, which any agent harness in the real world handles fine. (2) With baseline mis-measured low, OPD got an aggressive gradient signal at every step. With only 8 effective steps and loss in the 5-28 range, the 8 noisy updates moved the LoRA in inconsistent directions and destroyed the model. The right disposition was never iter 1 with this rubric \u2014 it was 'inspect responses, fix rubric, re-baseline.'", "next_focus": "Retire cap #5 here. Baseline 0.85 is in the OPD sweet spot but headroom is only 0.15 \u2014 even a perfect-ceiling adapter would lift composite by at most 0.15pp. Better to capture the eval-design-can-fail-in-BOTH-directions lesson and move to cap #6. Backport the lesson to the SFT and OPD skills: low baseline (<0.30) is a signal to INSPECT RESPONSES before training, not a green light for aggressive OPD."}, "kiln_polish_noted": true, "git_sha": "555dc5b7", "notes": "The \u00a710 all-zeros gate (composite < 0.5\u00d7baseline) would have fired with the corrected rubric (0.0983 < 0.425). Failure_mode.md not written because the failure is rubric-design, not adapter-broken; the adapter IS broken but the root cause is upstream."}
{"iter": 2, "slug": "h1-r8-lr5e5-2ep-spp2", "ts": "2026-05-18T06:32:46.238550Z", "status": "discarded", "family": "H1", "target_sub_score": "target_intent_captured", "hypothesis": "Conservative dosage (rank 8, lr 5e-5, spp 2) preserves baseline while lifting target_intent_captured \u22653pp.", "verdict": "\u2717 Falsified \u2014 best checkpoint ckpt-25 = 0.5217 (-32.83pp vs baseline 0.85). All 3 checkpoints regressed. Gentler settings AVOIDED iter 1's catastrophic 0.10 collapse (good) but training still degraded the model. Inspection revealed the deep failure mode: at high baseline (0.85), student-teacher rollout overlap is variable \u2014 some rollouts perfect, some malformed. OPD locks in BOTH kinds, so each malformed-rollout step is a regression that good-rollout steps can't compensate for.", "composite": 0.5217, "composite_delta": -0.3283, "headroom_used": -2.189, "sub_scores": {"strict_format": 0.5667, "applies_cleanly": 0.5667, "target_intent_captured": 0.4167, "minimal_changes": 0.5667}, "regressions": [{"sub_score": "strict_format", "delta": -0.4333, "severity": "major"}, {"sub_score": "applies_cleanly", "delta": -0.3, "severity": "major"}, {"sub_score": "target_intent_captured", "delta": -0.35, "severity": "major"}, {"sub_score": "minimal_changes", "delta": -0.3, "severity": "major"}], "training": {"rank": 8, "alpha": 16, "lr": 5e-05, "epochs": 2, "samples_per_prompt": 2, "max_tokens": 512, "top_k": 8, "effective_steps": 57, "nominal_steps": 96, "skip_rate": 0.406, "elapsed_s": 258, "checkpoints_saved": ["25", "50"]}, "asi": {"what_worked": "Gentler settings avoided iter 1's catastrophic 0.10 collapse \u2014 ckpt-25 landed at 0.52, much better than iter 1's 0.10. Rollout averaging (spp=2) and lower lr/rank do dampen the per-step damage. The kiln checkpoint feature continues to deliver \u2014 we have ckpt-25 vs ckpt-50 vs final to characterize the regression trajectory.", "what_failed": "OPD regresses a baseline-0.85 model regardless of settings. Inspection of ckpt-25: some prompts produce baseline-identical clean diffs, others produce malformed diffs (`a/loop.py` instead of `--- a/loop.py`, or `print(i) \u2192 print(i)` no-op replacements). The mechanism: at high baseline, student-teacher overlap is variable. Some student rollouts match teacher; OPD on those is a no-op. Some student rollouts are malformed (the 15% failure tail in baseline); reverse-KL makes the student MORE confident in those malformed outputs. The asymmetry: good-rollout steps don't help (already correct); bad-rollout steps actively hurt. Net: regression with high variance.", "next_focus": "This is the DEEP finding of cap #5. **OPD has a high-baseline failure mode when student-teacher rollout overlap is variable.** Skill \u00a70 says OPD helps in baseline (0.4, 0.95); the upper bound was arbitrary. Cap #5 sharpens it: at baseline ~0.85 with variable rollouts, OPD regresses regardless of settings. The fix is a DIFFERENT training paradigm: H6 (off-policy SFT cold-start) shows the student CORRECT teacher rollouts explicitly via supervised learning, narrowing the gap, before OPD refines. Without H6 infra, the best adapter for cap #5 is NO ADAPTER \u2014 the baseline 4B is already 0.85 and OPD can only hurt it."}, "kiln_polish_noted": true, "git_sha": "697b43ae", "notes": "Best 'adapter' for cap #5 is the base model (composite 0.85, no adapter). OPD findings: (1) iter 1 catastrophic, (2) iter 2 gentler-but-still-regressing, (3) the mechanism is variable rollout overlap at high baseline. Backport finding to skill \u00a70."}
{"iter": 3, "slug": "h6-sft-coldstart", "ts": "2026-05-18T07:34:28.416240Z", "status": "discarded", "family": "H6", "target_sub_score": "target_intent_captured", "hypothesis": "SFT on 24 teacher-generated diffs lifts composite above baseline 0.85.", "verdict": "\u2717 Falsified \u2014 plateaued at 0.79 across 2ep and 4ep runs. SFT loss converged cleanly to 0.005 (memorization confirmed) but eval composite hit a ceiling 6pp below baseline. 24 examples insufficient to generalize past surface-form memorization. The path that WOULD work is 100+ teacher rollouts.", "composite": 0.79, "composite_delta": -0.06, "headroom_used": -0.4, "sub_scores": {"strict_format": 1.0, "applies_cleanly": 0.7667, "target_intent_captured": 0.7667, "minimal_changes": 0.7667}, "regressions": [{"sub_score": "applies_cleanly", "delta": -0.1, "severity": "minor", "note": "vs baseline 0.867"}, {"sub_score": "minimal_changes", "delta": -0.1, "severity": "minor", "note": "vs baseline 0.867"}], "training": {"trainer": "kiln cuda_sft_file generic", "data_size": 24, "data_source": "27B teacher (qwen3.6-27b-awq) via vLLM with enable_thinking=False", "epochs": 4, "checkpoints_saved": ["24", "48", "72"], "elapsed_s_total": 557, "final_loss": 0.005}, "asi": {"what_worked": "Generation pipeline + SFT trainer end-to-end. Teacher diffs were clean (mean 0.85 on training prompts, 20/24 perfect). SFT loss converged cleanly from 5 to 0.005 in 96 steps. Format learning was perfect (strict_format=1.0 across all SFT checkpoints) \u2014 SFT IS good at teaching surface form. The kiln cuda_sft_file trainer + new checkpoint feature worked first-try with no kiln changes needed.", "what_failed": "24 training examples is too few to generalize past memorization. The model learns the 24 specific patterns but the eval has 30 different prompts with varied surface forms. Composite plateaus at 0.79 \u2014 the model can produce a clean diff but makes small structural errors (wrong @@ line counts, swapped content) on prompts it didn't memorize. More epochs don't help (4ep ckpt-72 = 4ep final = 0.79). Loss dropped to 0.005 but eval was stuck.", "next_focus": "Cap #5 retires at baseline 0.85 (no adapter wins). The path to actually extract uplift from 27B on diffs is a teacher SFT corpus of ~100+ diverse prompts \u2014 not session-scoped. Captured as the third deep finding for the skill: for high-baseline capabilities, small SFT corpora HURT generalization."}, "kiln_polish_noted": true, "git_sha": "f6dd5f15", "notes": "Two-stage finding (2ep then 4ep) \u2014 same plateau both times confirms the ceiling is data-size limited, not training-time limited."}
